# {{.Name}}

This folder is a [Neptuno](https://github.com/robertoplancarte/neptuno)
project: a development environment for several repos, run with docker
compose, overmind and tmux. Run every command below from this folder.

## Layout

- `repos/<repo>/`: each repo's source, as a git submodule. Code changes go
  here, and each repo has its own git history, branches and guidelines (read
  its own `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md` before changing it).
- `dockerfiles/<repo>/`: the repo's development Dockerfile and its
  `entrypoint.sh`, which runs setup such as `bundle install` at every start.
- `procfiles/<repo>/Procfile`: the processes overmind runs for the repo.
- `environments/`: env files. `_shared/` applies to every repo, then
  `<repo>/base.env`, `<repo>/<env>.env` and git-ignored `*.local.env`.
  `neptuno env show <repo>` prints the merged result and where each value
  comes from.
- `compose.yml`: the containers. Shared services are reachable from the
  repos' containers by name: `postgres:5432`, `valkey:6379`, `s3:8333`,
  and the Svix webhook server at `svix:8071`.
- `Caddyfile`: the reverse proxy, which serves each repo at
  `http://<repo>.localhost` on this machine.
- `neptuno.yml`: project settings, such as repos that run on the host
  (`run: host`) and the MCP servers each repo serves.
- `.neptuno/`: generated on every `neptuno up`. Don't edit it.

## Running things

- `neptuno status` lists every repo and service with its state, processes,
  branch and commit.
- `neptuno up -d <repo>` starts a repo and everything it depends on.
  Always pass `-d`: without it Neptuno attaches to tmux and doesn't return.
- `neptuno down <repo>` stops a repo. Always name the repo: with no
  arguments it stops the whole project.
- `neptuno restart <repo>` restarts a repo's processes, and
  `neptuno restart <repo>:<process>` one of them.
- `docker compose logs --tail 100 <repo>` shows a container's output,
  including its `entrypoint.sh` setup.
- After changing a Dockerfile, run `docker compose build <repo>` and then
  `neptuno up -d <repo>`.

## Working inside a repo

Repos run in their containers, with their own language versions, packages
and env, so run their tools there rather than on this machine:

```sh
docker compose exec -T <repo> <command>    # such as: docker compose exec -T api bin/rails test
```

`-T` gives plain output without a terminal. A repo with `run: host` in
`neptuno.yml` runs on this machine instead: run its commands in
`repos/<repo>`.

## Handing work to Claude inside a repo's container

Each repo's container has Claude Code, signed in through `.neptuno/claude`.
For work that takes many steps inside one repo, hand it over and read the
answer:

```sh
docker compose exec -T <repo> claude -p "<task>" --mcp-config /root/.claude/neptuno-mcp.json
```

`--mcp-config` gives it the project's MCP servers at the addresses that work
inside containers. A person can open an interactive one with
`neptuno claude <repo>`.

## MCP servers

Repos declare the MCP servers they serve in `neptuno.yml`, under
`repos.<repo>.mcp`, with the path each answers on. `neptuno mcp` lists them
with the URL that works on this machine (`http://<repo>.localhost/...`) and
the one that works inside containers (`http://<repo>:<port>/...`).
`neptuno up` writes the first kind to `.mcp.json` here.
`neptuno mcp serve` offers all of this to MCP clients over stdio:
Neptuno's own tools (status, up, down, exec, logs, handing a task to a
repo's Claude) and every repo's MCP tools.

## Skills

`.claude/skills/` holds step-by-step guides for common jobs here: adding a
repo, debugging a service that won't come up, and handing a task to a
repo's Claude. Each `SKILL.md` starts with a description of when to use it.
